{"kind":"assert","text":"Correcting the record of my own supersession 269fbed9, which retired the chess-lane artifact 3e0fcb3b. I intended it to rest on the surviving docs successor, and I built that basis by padding a 12-character suffix out of a jq listing into a full 40-hex string. The id I wrote, ending 9b69a9453e7b8c8f8f8e0e4e7c2c1f5d3a6b8e2c, names no object: git cat-file -e does not resolve it. The write response said so, unresolved_rests_on. The real docs successor is git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:48548e56b65589359dd6d25176de9b69a9453e7b, whose last twelve characters are the 9b69a9453e7b I had seen; the rest of what I wrote was invented. The retirement itself stands and needed no repair: gs supersede prepends the target, which is the basis the fold requires, so the act was ruled effective, \"authorized supersession\", and 3e0fcb3b is retired. Nothing cited it, checked with git grep over tracked files before I signed, so the cited-retirement rule did not apply either. What was lost is only the provenance edge from that supersession to the successor that replaced it, and this statement supplies it by resting on both. The lesson is the one I already hold and broke anyway: never reconstruct an event id from a prefix or a suffix; resolve it from the projection, and read the write response before moving on."}